00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.080
So how does Istio work?

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.980
You may have heard of this
concept of a service mesh,

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.300
right?

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.370
There are many definitions
of service mesh.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.080
This is one.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.810
Picture all of your workloads
running on a platform,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.280
such as Kubernetes.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.010
The idea behind a service mesh
is it is a common substrate

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.290
that lives on top
of your workloads--

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.120
does not modify them--

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.760
and through something
like a set of proxies,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.210
allows you, the operator, to
manage network interactions

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.610
in a very consistent way.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.860
This is what a service mesh is.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.500
Istio is one implementation
of this paradigm.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.140
There are multiple open-source
service mesh tools out there.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.100
Istio is one.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.310
It was created a few years ago
by Google and in partnership

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.800
with companies such
as IBM and Red Hat.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.050
It is fully open-source.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:08.210
But I think that this
definition sort of undersells

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.660
what Istio is, really.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.750
I think what Istio actually
is is it's a platform.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.300
And you're like, oh my gosh,
no, not another platform, right?

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.060
Kubernetes is a platform.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.370
But I really do think
that Istio is a platform.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.910
It lives on top of Kubernetes,
exposes a set of APIs

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.490
for you to do some
very powerful things.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.630
Raise your hand if
you've heard of Knative.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:35.840
Yeah, so Knative is this amazing
open-source serverless platform

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.990
that uses Istio,
works on top of Istio

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:44.330
to do really cool stuff with
simple serverless functions

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.420
on Kubernetes.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.630
That's super cool--
just one example

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.120
of how you can build
on top of Istio.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.830
So I wanted to
say that outright.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:52.770
How does it work?

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.370
And I'm sorry if this
is review for you.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.080
But the way Istio
works is through a set

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.440
of sidecar proxies.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.790
So this is the data plane.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.720
So in this example, we have
one Kubernetes cluster,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.300
a set of pods-- those
are the gray boxes.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.020
And inside of each pod,
we have two containers.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.000
So remember in
Kubernetes that a pod can

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.250
contain one or more containers.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.530
And the thing about
a pod in Kubernetes

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.410
is that they share the same
Linux network interface.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.290
What this means is that
containers in the same pod

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.970
can talk to each
other over local host.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.540
So this is how the
magic of Istio works.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.250
And what happens is you
use Istio to inject proxies

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.300
into your workloads.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.050
And then all subsequent
traffic, inbound and outbound,

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.410
goes through those proxies.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.850
And you're like, OK, so what?

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.160
Like, proxies, great.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.710
Well, this is what Istio does.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.170
Istio configures these
proxies using rules

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:52.650
that you give it to do very
customizable things with Envoy.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.490
And I want to go
back to just mention

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.670
that those pink hexagons--
what exactly is that proxy?

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.440
It is the Envoy proxy.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.370
So this is an open-source
layer 7 high-performance proxy

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:04.770
written in C++.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.660
Istio is not the only service
mesh tool that uses Envoy.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.080
You can use Envoy on your own.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.380
Some of you might even have
written a control plane

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:14.380
that configures Envoy proxies.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.090
That is the job of Istio.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:22.070
One key thing to note here is
that Istio installs its API

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.262
on Kubernetes as CRDs.

00:05:24.262 --> 00:05:27.210
CRD stands for custom
resource definition.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.330
So what this means is
that you can interact

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:32.070
with your Istio policies, and
your rules, and all of that

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:36.020
using kubectl using
Kubernetes native tooling.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.390
And this becomes
relevant when you're

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.420
trying to do things like
continuous deployment for Istio

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.740
resources, and you want
a good way to do it.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.742
Put it all together, we have
the full Istio architecture.

00:05:49.742 --> 00:05:51.200
At the top, we have
the data plane.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.630
At the bottom, we have
the control plane.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:56.450
The key thing to note here
is that the Envoy voice side

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:58.745
cars can handle multiple
different types of traffic.

00:05:58.745 --> 00:06:00.620
So remember, so this is
layer seven, so we're

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.720
talking HTTP, GRPC, that layer,
the application layer, that

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:06.710
is to say.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.810
And it's all running
in Kubernetes.

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:10.850
Again, you can run
workloads in VMs.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:11.930
Istio can handle it.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.650
But unfortunately, don't
have the time today.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.500
I'm here today mostly to
talk about configuring Pilot.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.820
Issue has multiple
control plane components.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.620
So we have Galley
for config ingestion.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.860
We have Citadel for cert
management, for MTLS.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.310
And we have Mixer for telemetry.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.590
These are all crucial
components for Istio.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.920
Mainly the way that
you configure traffic

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:35.840
is indirectly through Pilot.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.140
And Pilot pushes your
config, your traffic rules

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.095
down to the Envoy proxies
in the form of filters.

00:06:42.095 --> 00:06:44.828
So you, the operator, are
never interacting directly

00:06:44.828 --> 00:06:45.620
with these proxies.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:49.482
You talk to Istio,
the control plane.

00:06:49.482 --> 00:06:51.690
And you might be kind of
wondering, like OK, so what?

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:55.550
What's the actual added value of
taking on a piece of complexity

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.230
such as Istio?

00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:01.910
It's not the world's most
easy thing to just deploy it.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.560
What does it get you?

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.730
A whole lot for
traffic management.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.340
So one, just basic visibility
into which microservice

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:10.743
is calling which.

00:07:10.743 --> 00:07:12.410
You might have
experienced this yourself

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:16.130
if you're trying to decouple
a monolith into microservices

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:19.222
introduces a whole bunch
of problems such as, all

00:07:19.222 --> 00:07:20.680
of a sudden, you
have new services,

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.850
making network
calls to each other

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.850
that maybe they
weren't doing before.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.120
Traffic inspection, so
taking individual requests

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.060
and making decisions based
on things like HTTP headers.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:34.610
I'm going to demo
this in just a moment.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.840
Granular
percentage-based routing.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:41.060
So the thing about a Kubernetes
service, plain old service,

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.828
is that you are limited
by your workload scaling.

00:07:43.828 --> 00:07:46.370
And what I mean by this is say
you have a Kubernetes service,

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:49.100
and behind that sits
a bunch of pods,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:51.410
and you want to do
a Canary deployment,

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:54.110
and you have, let's say,
one pod per deployment,

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:57.170
the best you could do is 50-50.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.720
There would be no way in
Kubernetes plain to just say,

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:03.180
I want 1% of traffic to go
to that new Canary version

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.180
so I could roll back
really quickly if something

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.630
goes wrong.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:07.780
So there's that.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.070
Automation-- this is, I think,
a really, really key thing.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.550
If you take away anything
from this talk, and I hope

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:14.570
it's this.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:19.555
Because Istio exposes APIs that
you can add yamls to and use

00:08:19.555 --> 00:08:20.930
all of the tooling
that you would

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.030
be using with plain
old Kubernetes,

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.970
you can do an
essential automation

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.290
that you could not do before.

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:30.110
So I'm talking like turn on
MTLS end-to-end encryption

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.450
for your entire mesh
with a single rule.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:34.789
That's pretty amazing, right?

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:36.260
And finally,
decouple the network

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.163
from your application code.

00:08:38.163 --> 00:08:40.580
What this means is that if
you're moving to microservices,

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:42.929
you might have multiple
programming languages.

00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:44.480
Now you might have
a lot of services

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.070
to deal with, a lot of
teams owning these services.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.080
With Istio, you no
longer have to have

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:53.290
your developers think about
things like firewall rules

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.630
or retry logic.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:58.750
The idea of Istio is to take
all of that network logic

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.880
and to put it in the
hands of the operators

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.310
so that you can manage
it in a unified way.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:06.400
And I know this is
maybe a mental shift

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.310
from thinking about the
way we do these things,

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.320
and it's incremental.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.300
And I'll mention that
at the end as well.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.510
OK, that is how Istio works
in a very small nutshell.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:21.050
What I'm going to do now is get
into the fun part, the demos.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.920
Traffic rules.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.540
So, how do you tell Istio to do
things with the Sidecar proxy?

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.410
This is the question, right?

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:33.420
Istio exposes a traffic API.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.220
It exposes multiple objects.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.540
Here are four.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.180
Today, we'll mainly
just be talking about.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.580
The first is a destination rule.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:42.780
What's a destination rule?

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:46.650
It is a way to group a service
into subsets, which is just

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.040
a long version of saying, you
can take a Kubernetes service

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.360
and partition it
however you want.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.950
So in this example, I'm about
to show doing a partition based

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:56.870
on versions.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:59.490
But you could have something
like here's my prod front end,

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:01.020
and here's my build front end.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:02.350
You could do that.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:06.820
And this destination rule
backs a virtual service.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.830
And this is really
the key traffic rule,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:14.050
API object in Istio
that you should know.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:16.830
A virtual service is the
way that you list rules.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.860
So in this example
here, this yaml,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:23.250
I am going to do a
traffic splitting

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.440
demo in which I canary--

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.690
or blue/green is really
the operative term--

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.270
of a second version of my
front-end microservice.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.878
And I'm going to direct
10% of traffic to it.

00:10:33.878 --> 00:10:35.670
This is sort of the
"Hello World" of Istio,

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.950
so I apologize if you've
seen this a million times.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:40.030
But just to get
our feet wet here,

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:43.140
it's going to be the
first demo I show.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.080
So what's the value of doing
this granular percentage-based

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.540
routing that Istio lets you do?

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.960
You can scale, and you
can release new versions

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.800
and rollback quickly just
through the magic of Kubernetes

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.470
Delete yaml.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.990
So that's pretty neat.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.180
Today in the demos,
I'm going to be

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.850
showing I will be using
the Hipster Shop demo app.

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.700
You may have seen this before in
some of the other Google demos.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:12.270
The only reason that we
use this demo over others

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.300
is that we tried to
more simulate something

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.410
you might actually be
running in production.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:20.400
So it's an e-commerce
application with a front end

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.670
and a series of
back-end microservices.

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:27.030
So traffic splitting, let's
get into the demo, shall we?

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.640
What I will be showing,
again, 90% of traffic

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:33.300
will be going to V1, 10% to V2.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.920
So let's head over
to our demo here.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:42.880
Can everyone see this OK?

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:44.370
Do I need to zoom in more?

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.330
So what-- just to
get a lay of the land

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:52.940
here, what I'm
showing on the right

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:56.250
is just a set of repeated
curl commands to a version end

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.790
point of the front end.

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:00.300
So I currently have one version
of my front end deployed

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:01.810
to my Kubernetes cluster.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:04.650
This is just plain old
GKE with plain old Istio

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.030
installed on it.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:10.810
And I am sending 100 requests
to the front end service.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.210
So this is that
Kubernetes DNS name, [?

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:18.700
frontend.serviceclusterlocal. ?]
So what I'm going to show here

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:21.670
is creating a destination
rule that breaks the front end

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:25.600
into two pieces, V1 and V2.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:28.840
And then I will be splitting
the traffic as I showed there

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:30.340
on the slides.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:32.810
So what we'll see
on the right here,

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.410
hopefully, is I'm going to
deploy the second version.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:36.550
And what we're going
to see right away

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:38.530
is that the default
Kubernetes round robin load

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:39.585
balancing takes effect.

00:12:39.585 --> 00:12:40.960
And what this
means is just we're

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:46.242
going to see 50-50, just random
selection between V1 and V2.

00:12:46.242 --> 00:12:47.950
And then I'll apply
the rule, and we will

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:49.690
watch 90% of traffic go to V2.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.500
We deploy V2.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:00.900
If I do a kubectl get pods
here, we can see that--

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:03.360
you saw little 503 there--

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.200
that the second version of
our front end is running.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:10.890
If I run this command again,
we've got front end V2 running,

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.350
and then we're going
to apply the resources.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:17.160
So apply the destination rule,
and apply the traffic splitting

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.950
rule.

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:21.420
And what we'll see here in
real time is that about 90%

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.430
of traffic is headed to V1.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.190
And we're doing a
blue/green on V2.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.260
Again, in a real
production use case,

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.470
hopefully you're using something
like a continuous deployment

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:35.160
tool to do this, but just for
demo purposes, basic traffic

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.630
splitting with Istio.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:39.452
Let's do something a little
bit more interesting here.

00:13:39.452 --> 00:13:40.785
So if we go back to the slides--

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:47.920
perfect-- let's look at
content-based routing.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.850
So I mentioned a few
minutes ago that Istio

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.110
can inspect requests
and make decisions

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.790
based on those requests.

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:56.280
So what I'm going
to be showing is

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:00.630
that you can use Istio to have
a rule that looks at the HTTP

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:03.270
headers and routes
to a back end based

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:06.120
on the contents of that header.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.890
Why would you want to do this?

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.230
There would be potentially
a lot of reasons,

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:13.140
like traffic prioritization.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.690
Building an A/B testing
framework on top of Istio

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:17.650
would be an example.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:23.220
So what I'm going to show
is that any request that has

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.070
a foo:bar1 header,
for demo purposes,

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:27.720
will go to front end V1.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:32.570
And all other requests
will go to front end V2.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:37.147
So if we go back to
the terminal here,

00:14:37.147 --> 00:14:38.730
I'm going to reuse
the setup I already

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:43.020
had from the blue/green demo.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:47.100
And here is the rule
that allows you to do

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.820
that content-based routing.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:53.070
So again, we're looking at a
virtual service, Istio CRD,

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:56.160
and rather than doing
just plain old splitting,

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:00.810
we're going to do a match on
the header with an exact match

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:01.860
foo:bar1.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.140
And we're going to
route all requests that

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.650
fit that match to V1.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:10.580
And then we will route
all other requests to V2.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:12.650
Notice the kind
of the precedence

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.773
that is implicit in this yaml.

00:15:15.773 --> 00:15:17.690
They say you shouldn't
build logic into yamls.

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:20.640
This virtual service does
in which we fall through.

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.910
So if we don't fit
this match condition,

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:26.020
we fall through to V2.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:29.300
So here on the right,
when I apply this rule,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.770
and it's a plain curl
request with no headers, what

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:35.660
do you think will happen?

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.310
Raise your hand
if you think V1--

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:40.250
it will be V1, 100%.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:42.990
Raise your hand if
it will be 100% V2.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:44.375
OK, great.

00:15:44.375 --> 00:15:47.150
Just making sure you listen.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.100
So we're going to
apply this resource.

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.870
In place, we're going to modify
the virtual service we already

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:52.370
had.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.520
So 100% of requests went to V2.

00:15:58.520 --> 00:15:59.020
Why?

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:02.230
Because we don't have
that HTTP request header.

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:03.220
Here I am in a pod.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:05.320
And I can curl the front end.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:10.480
With that foo:bar1 header,
[? the ?] version end point.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:11.410
And we will see V1.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:14.200
And just to show I'm not faking
here, I can run this again,

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:17.870
and again, and again,
and we will see V1.

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:22.370
Run it without the header,
and we will see V2.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:26.690
So again, demo purposes,
showing as best as I can

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:30.200
kind of the things you
can do on top of Istio.

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:31.820
So if we go back to
the slides here--

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.010
so for all the subsequent
demos in this section,

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:44.010
I'm actually going to
shift my focus from things

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:47.160
like load balancing to building
resilient microservices

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:48.480
with Istio.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:52.200
So in the following demo,
I will be showing something

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:53.370
called circuit breaking.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:55.360
Raise your hand if you've
heard of circuit breaking.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:55.860
Yeah.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:17:00.870
So this is a very common best
practice for microservices

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:02.210
and for distributed systems--

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.500
Istio did not invent this--

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:09.780
in which you wrap your services
in a piece of logic, or circuit

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:15.660
breaker, such that if you have
multiple failing requests,

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.790
essentially you're
applying back pressure

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:19.290
to avoid cascading failures.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:20.640
And it helps.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.190
So what we're going
to show is if we have

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:27.450
multiple concurrent requests
and more than one fail,

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:29.310
we're going to flip
the circuit breaker,

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:31.410
or trip the circuit
breaker rather,

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:34.920
so that we fail right
away, and we don't actually

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:38.213
make the full call
to our front end.

00:17:38.213 --> 00:17:39.630
And what this--
yeah, so what this

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.830
helps you do is if you wrap all
of your services in this logic,

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:44.610
you're building a
more resilient mesh.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:47.170
And we all want that.

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:48.870
So what this actual
demo will show

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:53.200
is I'm actually running a load
generator pod in my cluster.

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:55.740
And I will be sending
multiple concurrent requests

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:56.550
to the front end.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.620
And we're going to
watch some of them

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:01.510
fail after we apply the rule.

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:05.400
So I know it's kind of
a demo, but let's head

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:07.384
back to the terminal here.

00:18:07.384 --> 00:18:12.200
So I've got multiple
clusters running.

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:14.750
I'm actually going to go to
a new cluster for this demo.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:18.515
And let's see here--

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:22.050
all right.

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:30.170
I want to make sure I'm showing
this rule as best as I can.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.750
So the way you configure
a circuit breaker in Istio

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:34.130
is with a destination rule.

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:35.930
And you're like, wait, we've
been doing virtual services

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:36.470
this whole time.

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:37.640
What's a destination rule?

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:40.820
It's a little bit strange, but
basically a destination rule

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.355
applies to inbound traffic.

00:18:42.355 --> 00:18:44.480
So what we're doing is
we're wrapping the front end

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:48.752
service in a destination
rule such that--

00:18:48.752 --> 00:18:52.350
and I want to make sure I'm
showing this as best as I can--

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:56.550
such that if we have more than
one concurrent connection,

00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:00.330
client connection to the front
end, and more than one of them

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:03.120
is in pending state, we're
going to start kicking out

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.640
subsequent client connections.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.920
And this is Envoy
logic, so you can

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:09.480
go ahead and read
about the way Envoy

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:10.980
implements outlier detection.

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:12.120
Istio builds on that.

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:15.240
It doesn't really
reinvent the wheel at all.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.700
The key thing to
note is that we are

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:21.108
going to set that time out
period for three minutes.

00:19:21.108 --> 00:19:23.150
And I'm not going to show
the full three minutes.

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:27.810
But, yeah, so that's
circuit breaker.

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:32.660
So what I'm going
to do here is run

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:36.280
multiple concurrent
requests into the front end.

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:40.680
So in this example, I'm going to
do 10 concurrent requests, 100

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:42.117
total requests.

00:19:42.117 --> 00:19:43.950
And we're going to watch
all of these paths,

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:45.975
hopefully, and get two 100s.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.870
So we can see here that all of
the requests to the front end

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:53.787
passed, which is good.

00:19:53.787 --> 00:19:55.870
You want more than
10 clients to be

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.360
able to talk to your web
front end at once, ideally.

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:02.311
And we're going to apply
the destination rule,

00:20:02.311 --> 00:20:05.340
and we're going to run
that command again.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:08.030
And so what's happening
is Envoy knows

00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:11.900
to kick a 503 error in the case
that the circuit breaker is

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:13.310
tripped.

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:15.920
So what we're going to see here
in the terminal on the right

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:21.110
is a whole bunch
of 503s hopefully.

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:23.940
So a parsed non OK
code, 503, HTTP.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:25.070
So that came from Envoy.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:29.120
And you'll note-- so with 10
concurrent requests, only 15%

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.770
of them actually went
through to the front end.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:34.910
Again, in production, you would
build a more robust system

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:35.750
around testing this.

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:38.750
This is just, again,
for demo purposes.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:39.890
So that's circuit breaker.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.150
I've got one last demo in
this traffic rules section.

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.020
Thank you for
bearing with me here.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:48.450
If we go back to the slides,
the last demo in the section,

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:51.290
I'm going to show
fault injection.

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:52.790
So, raise your hand
if you've heard

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:54.590
of chaos testing in production?

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:55.130
Yeah.

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:57.890
So the premise here is that
traditional unit integration

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:00.140
smoke tests cannot
predict every failure case

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:01.400
in your microservices.

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.550
You would ideally like a
system or a framework that

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:06.710
is prodding at your
mesh and trying

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:11.180
to find weak areas so that you
can build up that resilience.

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:15.230
So the idea here is that Istio
allows you to inject faults

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:17.973
into one edge in your topology.

00:21:17.973 --> 00:21:19.640
And so what I mean
by that is what we're

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:22.820
going to do in this demo
is inject a HTTP 400

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:27.290
bad request error in a specific
connection between checkout

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:28.760
and product catalog.

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:30.980
Notice we are not
touching the requests

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:33.780
that are going from front
end to product catalog.

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:37.250
So this is another example of
how Istio allows you to really

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:41.360
granularly choose what
parts of traffic apply

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:42.840
and what services.

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:47.320
Again, yeah, controlled
disruptions into the mesh.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:49.190
So, let's go ahead
and demo that.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:52.040
If we go back to
the terminal here,

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:53.750
switch over to a new cluster.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:05.170
So the way that you
do fault injection

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:09.760
is with both a destination
rule and a virtual service.

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:13.390
Let's see here, all right.

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:15.920
So let me show that.

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:18.140
The destination rule, all
it does-- so again, we're

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:21.780
introducing a disruption between
checkout and product catalog.

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:25.160
So what we're going to
do is define a subset

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:26.900
for product catalog V1.

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:32.840
And the virtual service needs
this to inject that fault.

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:35.600
And so notice like with the
content-based routing demo,

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.928
what we're doing is
this match condition.

00:22:37.928 --> 00:22:39.470
But rather than
matching on a header,

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:41.540
we're matching on
the source workload.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:45.440
So we're saying, OK, only
requests from checkout

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:49.370
should have this
400 status code.

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:51.710
Abort those requests,
100% of them.

00:22:51.710 --> 00:22:55.110
Everything else fall
through to normal behavior.

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:56.360
So that's what we hope to see.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:58.110
And the way I'm going
to demo this, again,

00:22:58.110 --> 00:22:59.990
just for example
purposes, is by going

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:02.870
into the actual application
into the front end

00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:04.190
and trying to do a checkout.

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:06.840
And it hopefully will fail.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:11.100
So let's see here, all right.

00:23:13.573 --> 00:23:15.240
So we're going to go
into our front end.

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:19.160
Oops, that was not
what we wanted.

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:20.940
So let's see here.

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:22.375
Here's our external IP.

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:28.270
So, here's the Hipster
Shop front end.

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:31.000
And what we're going to do
is try to do a checkout.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:34.150
So notice how we injected the
fault into product catalog,

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.280
but we can still see
the list of products.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.655
This is because, again, the
connections between front end

00:23:38.655 --> 00:23:40.690
and product catalog
are still OK.

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:45.220
But if we tried to do a
checkout, place your order,

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:48.150
we will see an error that says
internal, failed to prepare

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:50.130
order, failed to get product.

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:56.250
It's a 500, but internally
it is a 400 HTTP error.

00:23:56.250 --> 00:24:00.090
And what we're going to do
is if I delete these rules

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:06.620
and I try to actually redo
that checkout transaction,

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:07.940
it'll work.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:09.500
So again, basic
fault injection, you

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.660
would usually apply
these sorts of resources

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:15.020
in the context of a larger,
more controlled experiment.

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:16.520
In production, I
would not advise

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:18.470
testing the resiliency
of your applications

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:22.550
by applying these rules to
your production cluster,

00:24:22.550 --> 00:24:24.340
maybe for obvious reasons.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:27.335
So with that, we end
the first section.

00:24:27.335 --> 00:24:28.460
Thanks for bearing with me.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:34.290
If we go back to
the slides, perfect.

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:39.710
So that was kind of a whirlwind
tour of East-West traffic

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:40.220
routing.

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:42.710
And the key here is
to give you a taste

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:45.260
of how you can build on
top of the Istio APIs

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:48.890
to do cool things.

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:51.290
So in the last two
sections, I'm going

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:55.100
to cover Ingress and Egress.

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:58.890
So traffic in first.

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.140
Raise your hand if you're
running something like NGINX

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:03.540
or a reverse proxy in
front of your services?

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:04.200
Yeah, right.

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:07.170
So inbound and Ingress
traffic into Kubernetes

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:10.560
or into a data center
is maybe nothing

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:12.450
new to anyone in this room.

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:14.970
The thing I would like you
to take away from this slide

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:18.450
is that Istio has a very
deliberate model in which

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:21.470
everything is an Envoy proxy.

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:24.270
So what is the
value out of that?

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:26.600
It means you can configure
East-West traffic

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:30.620
in the same way you configure
inbound and outbound,

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:32.300
with the same yamls.

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:34.430
And this becomes
very powerful when

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:40.400
you're talking about things
like MTLS and traffic splitting.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:43.760
And yeah, so what I'm
going to show here

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:47.000
is how exactly that's working.

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.880
The way that Istio
Ingress works by default

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:52.790
is that there is a default
Ingress gateway and Envoy

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:56.750
proxy that is running
and is exposed internally

00:25:56.750 --> 00:26:00.140
through an external IP
on a Kubernetes cluster

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:04.430
so that you configure it to
punch a port in that Ingress

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:06.470
gateway to allow traffic in.

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:08.885
And I've actually been
doing this this entire time.

00:26:08.885 --> 00:26:10.760
So I'm going to show
how that's been working.

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:14.180
Like how am I accessing this
web front end in my browser?

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:16.730
It's with this exact
set of resources.

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:18.860
My client, my laptop
right here, is

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:21.950
going through the Ingress
Gateway configured via gateway

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:25.460
yaml, going to an
Ingress virtual service,

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:28.950
finally landing at the
front end, back and--

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:32.610
the front end,
front end back end.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.070
So here's the Gateway yaml
that allows us to configure

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:36.300
that Ingress gateway.

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:39.540
So we have a selector
here that says,

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:42.630
I'm talking about the default
Istio Ingress gateway.

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:44.340
You can deploy your own.

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:46.650
But I'm basically
punching a port, port 80

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:48.990
and the default Istio
Ingress gateway,

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:53.460
and routing to any host
I configure it with.

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:56.620
And in a lot of demos, you'll
see this host wildcard star.

00:26:56.620 --> 00:27:00.855
In production, you would be more
specific about the front end.

00:27:00.855 --> 00:27:02.230
So I'm just going
to quickly show

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:04.120
those yamls in my terminal.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:06.520
I'm not going to deploy
anything new or special.

00:27:09.265 --> 00:27:14.230
Let's see here,
all right, perfect.

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:21.150
So let me show you the
actual Ingress gateway

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:22.420
pod that's running.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:23.880
So when you install Istio--

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:26.190
so these are all of the Istio
control plane components

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:28.197
that are running in my cluster.

00:27:28.197 --> 00:27:30.030
And you'll notice this
Istio Ingress gateway

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:32.010
pod, this one out of one.

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:35.040
So this is a single
Envoy container

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.980
that is running that
we will configure.

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:40.950
And we configure it like this,
through this Gateway resource.

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:44.130
These are the same resources
I just showed on the slides.

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:45.930
And these are all
applied to the cluster.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.840
I can do a kubectl get gateway.

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:49.360
Here it is.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:51.995
I can do a kubectl
get virtual service.

00:27:51.995 --> 00:27:53.370
I can actually
show you that one.

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:58.240
And we're saying for
inbound hosts that

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.240
have made it to the
virtual service,

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:03.760
route to the back end
running on port 80, which

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:06.140
is where the Kubernetes
service is running.

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:10.990
And what I can do from there
is get the Istio Kubernetes

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:15.940
services, get the external
IP of the Ingress gateway,

00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:17.920
and this is how we
get to the front end.

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:22.060
So in production, you would be
mapping a well-known DNS name

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.500
to this IP.

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:26.950
And it's a stable IP that
you can use to back--

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:30.230
or to front, rather,
multiple services.

00:28:30.230 --> 00:28:35.240
So if I go to this IP, I should
be able to see the Hipster Shop

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:36.800
front end.

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:39.860
So that's a really quick
rundown of Ingress.

00:28:39.860 --> 00:28:42.530
There are ways to say-- sorry,
we can go back to the slides

00:28:42.530 --> 00:28:44.070
actually.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:44.910
Thank you.

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:47.940
So there are ways to configure
that Ingress gateway to do

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:51.210
things enforce mutual TLS.

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:52.930
There is a wonderful
demo out there

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:55.440
that shows securing
the Ingress gateway

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:02.040
with Let's Encrypt or any sort
of cert issuer of your choice.

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:04.560
And yeah, I don't have
time today to showcase

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:10.650
that, but yeah, I can
find a way to link it.

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:12.660
So that's Ingress traffic.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:16.500
Outbound traffic works
in a similar way in Istio

00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:21.840
in that you can choose
to route outbound traffic

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:23.370
through an Envoy proxy.

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:25.530
It should be said that
in Istio 1.1, which

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.720
was released a
couple of weeks ago,

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:31.920
the Egress gateway I believe
is now disabled by default.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:35.040
Not everyone wants all traffic
to go through an Envoy proxy

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:36.510
as it's leaving the mesh.

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:38.310
There are very
valid reasons why.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:39.850
But here is the basic model.

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:41.433
So when you want
traffic-- so, I guess

00:29:41.433 --> 00:29:42.558
I should preface with this.

00:29:42.558 --> 00:29:44.760
Has anyone ever installed
Istio and all of a sudden

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.950
had things like your MySQL
connections just start to fail?

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:49.730
Yeah, right.

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:54.110
So by default, Istio locks
down outbound traffic.

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:55.410
This may change in the future.

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:57.530
But right now, if you've
encountered this problem,

00:29:57.530 --> 00:29:58.820
that is why.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:00.030
You need a yaml.

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.040
You need a service
entry that says,

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:04.370
yes, it's OK for my
traffic to go to MySQL,

00:30:04.370 --> 00:30:05.930
and this is where it is.

00:30:05.930 --> 00:30:09.980
Think of a service entry
as a piece of inventory

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:12.710
of a well-known
hostname that you are

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:15.820
allowing outbound traffic to.

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:17.370
And you add one of
these for anything

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:19.320
that needs-- your
in-mesh services need

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:21.030
to access outside.

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:27.263
So this would be like an API,
like the Google GCE APIs.

00:30:27.263 --> 00:30:29.430
And when you add one of
these resources, by default,

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:32.020
it goes directly to
that external service

00:30:32.020 --> 00:30:35.065
from the services Envoy proxy.

00:30:38.380 --> 00:30:45.390
What I want to show as my final
demo here is a way to secure--

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.060
so Egress traffic more securely.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.340
So imagine that you have
a situation in which you

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:52.950
want to really carefully
monitor any traffic that

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:54.450
is leaving your environment.

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:58.890
So say you're running Istio
on-prem in a specific region,

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:00.540
and you want to
make sure that you

00:31:00.540 --> 00:31:04.690
can enforce or at least
inspect outbound traffic.

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:08.310
So what you can do is
input an Envoy proxy

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:12.060
in between your in-mesh service
and your external service

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:13.807
to do exactly that.

00:31:13.807 --> 00:31:15.390
So what I'm going
to show in this demo

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:20.700
is I'm going to configure
the Egress gateway in Istio

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:27.030
to look at all traffic
to this EU bank endpoint.

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:28.300
Why would you want to do this?

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:29.925
There's another really
good reason why,

00:31:29.925 --> 00:31:33.230
which is [? it ?] started--

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:36.050
I think it's an
increasing best practice

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:39.133
to think of in-mesh
services the same way

00:31:39.133 --> 00:31:40.550
as you would
out-of-mesh services.

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:43.040
And what I mean by this
is just because something

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.750
is running in your
cluster does not

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.240
mean you can
necessarily trust it.

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.460
Kubernetes has service
accounts, and then we have--

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:51.840
our back end, we have all of
these safe systems in place.

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:54.590
But that doesn't mean that
a malicious service can't

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:58.140
make its way into your
cluster with, for example,

00:31:58.140 --> 00:32:01.260
a Docker image with it's
kind of an erroneous tag.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:08.220
So that is why I think this
demo is somewhat relevant.

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:11.450
So what I'm going to be showing
here is that in our demo app,

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:16.340
one of our back ends talks to a
bank API to get exchange rates.

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:19.400
And we're going to put an Egress
gateway in between the currency

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:23.493
service and the API to
look at outbound traffic.

00:32:23.493 --> 00:32:25.160
I'm not going to show
this in this demo,

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:27.260
but what you could
do with this gateway

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:30.200
is configure it, just as we
did all the way up until now,

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:37.010
with rules, such as Enforce
MTL to the EU bank end point.

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:43.280
So with that, I will do this
final demo back in my terminal.

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:51.060
Oops, all right, perfect.

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:55.980
Let's get on the right
cluster here, awesome.

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:57.750
So let's look at
the resources that

00:32:57.750 --> 00:32:59.800
allow us to do exactly this.

00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:04.950
So right now, I just
have my plain demo app

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:07.770
deployed, no fancy rules.

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:10.470
No, traffic is not
going through an Egress.

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:15.570
And what I want to show
just to prove it's working

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:18.840
is to actually get the really
spammy logs of this Egress

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:20.766
gateway.

00:33:20.766 --> 00:33:23.760
And actually-- yeah, sure.

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:25.615
I apologize if this
hurts anyone's eyes.

00:33:32.777 --> 00:33:34.360
So what we're going
to hopefully see--

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:36.568
and I was running this a
little bit earlier, so sorry

00:33:36.568 --> 00:33:38.320
for the spoiler alert.

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:40.510
But we're going
to watch requests

00:33:40.510 --> 00:33:43.660
go through the Egress
gateway via the logs

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:46.210
once I enable these rules.

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:49.060
What rules am I about to enable?

00:33:49.060 --> 00:33:49.960
These.

00:33:49.960 --> 00:33:53.080
So the way that you would
get this demo working

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.870
is you would configure
the Egress gateway, which

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:58.180
is already running because
I'm running a slightly

00:33:58.180 --> 00:33:59.710
dated version of Istio.

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:03.490
You would configure this
Egress gateway to route traffic

00:34:03.490 --> 00:34:05.620
to the external endpoint.

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:08.659
And then from there, you would
add additional resources.

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:11.949
The first would be
ensure that traffic

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:17.710
goes through this secure gateway
by specifying route rules just

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:19.929
as we have been
throughout this demo.

00:34:19.929 --> 00:34:22.420
And sorry that they're
a little bit dense here.

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:25.330
So it's, I know, a
lot to grep visually.

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:31.090
And then we would define a
destination rule that, again,

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:34.510
is forcing traffic to go
instead of from currency

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:37.659
service directly to
the EU bank endpoint,

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:41.440
we are using route rules
to funnel that traffic

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:43.239
through the Egress gateway.

00:34:43.239 --> 00:34:47.020
And so what we will hopefully
see in the Egress gateway

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:50.900
logs is we will see a
warm-up log that says,

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:52.540
OK, I'm the Egress
gateway, and I

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:57.400
know that I can be talking
to the EU bank API.

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:59.800
And then we will see the
