00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:07.340
So yeah, that's securing
Egress traffic again.

00:36:07.340 --> 00:36:10.220
With demos, it can
be tough to show

00:36:10.220 --> 00:36:13.490
exactly how you would bring
this into your own environments.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:16.610
But I hope it at least
gives you a taste.

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:19.230
So with that, I'm going to go
back to the slides and wrap up.

00:36:19.230 --> 00:36:20.730
Thank you so much
for being with me.

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:23.720
I know it's a lot of demos
and a lot of yaml to stare at.

00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:26.060
All right, back to the slides.

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:28.880
Closing thoughts.

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:32.510
I want to stress that Istio
1.1 one was released a couple

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:34.200
of weeks ago, super exciting.

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:37.350
It was the result of a ton of
hard work in the community,

00:36:37.350 --> 00:36:39.890
not just by Google, but by
many other organizations

00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:43.070
and individuals to help
improve Istio's performance

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:45.240
and scalability.

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:47.360
So 1.0 was not too long ago.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:48.950
And now, we're in 1.1.

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:50.840
What this means is
that Istio is still

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:52.850
a relatively young technology.

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:54.950
Some things are changing.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:56.700
And it is out in the open.

00:36:56.700 --> 00:36:57.890
It's open source.

00:36:57.890 --> 00:37:01.010
But what I want
to, I guess, stress

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:04.940
is that these APIs are the
building blocks from which you

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:06.660
build other things.

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:09.050
So Istio might have internal
control plane changes

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:11.910
in the coming months, year.

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:14.270
The hope is that these API
objects become the things

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:18.810
that your organizations
build on to do cool stuff.

00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:20.270
So that's 1.1.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:23.270
What are some best practices for
bringing Istio into production?

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:25.490
This is just my two cents.

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:28.820
It would be to
start somewhat slow,

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:31.100
pick the one feature
that you really

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:32.660
want to bring to
your environment

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:35.460
or try out if you have
a sandbox cluster.

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:38.060
So this could be something
like a blue/green deployment.

00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:40.550
This could be enabling
MTLS, which I didn't even

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:42.140
cover in this talk.

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:45.920
And learn the CRD, like
learn the virtual service

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:48.950
for that feature,
and it becomes easier

00:37:48.950 --> 00:37:51.090
after that to do other things.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:53.780
There is a somewhat
steep learning curve

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:55.820
that I know I
encountered myself.

00:37:55.820 --> 00:37:58.900
So I would stress that.

00:37:58.900 --> 00:38:01.040
And from there,
gather the tool kit

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:03.330
that helps you figure
out when things go wrong.

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:06.230
So there's awesome open source
tools that do service graph.

00:38:06.230 --> 00:38:09.020
So kiali is one example.

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:12.720
And yeah, there's a command
line tool called Istio CTL.

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:15.740
If you've never encountered
that, it's on the docks.

00:38:15.740 --> 00:38:17.910
It can do things like
validate resources,

00:38:17.910 --> 00:38:21.260
check if MTLS is working,
look at the proxy status

00:38:21.260 --> 00:38:22.450
via the Envoy APIs.

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:24.830
It can do a ton of cool stuff.

00:38:24.830 --> 00:38:27.020
And then from there,
work your way up.

00:38:27.020 --> 00:38:29.270
It's definitely a
journey, similar perhaps

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:30.780
to adopting Kubernetes.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.870
I'm not trying to
say that it's easy

00:38:32.870 --> 00:38:36.770
because it's a bit of a road.

00:38:36.770 --> 00:38:38.870
And this talk did not
even, I don't think,

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:41.510
scratched really the surface
of what Istio can do.

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:47.000
API management is one example,
and user authentication

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:50.240
with JWT tokens are
back, unified policies

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:53.930
across services, which
services can access which,

00:38:53.930 --> 00:38:56.060
doing custom
telemetry forwarding

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:57.710
to the sync of your choice--

00:38:57.710 --> 00:39:00.380
Prometheus, Grafana,
hybrid environments,

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:03.290
mesh expansion to VMs,
multiple Kubernetes clusters.

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:06.350
I mean Istio is, I think, a
massive piece of technology

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:08.840
that can do a whole bunch
of really cool stuff.

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:10.980
And if this is
interesting for you,

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.520
yeah, I would encourage you
to check out the other talks

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:16.670
that I'll be showing on the
screen in a few moments.

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:18.770
I wanted to give a quick
callout to Anthos, which

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:23.600
was announced yesterday,
which is Google's

00:39:23.600 --> 00:39:26.910
hybrid multi-cloud offering.

00:39:26.910 --> 00:39:32.090
And I want to maybe
clarify that Istio

00:39:32.090 --> 00:39:34.190
plays a key role in Anthos.

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:36.778
The way Istio plays a
key role is via the APIs.

00:39:36.778 --> 00:39:39.320
So all the resources I've been
showing throughout this talk--

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:41.690
virtual service, gateway,
destination rule--

00:39:41.690 --> 00:39:45.340
all of those can be used to
configure your mesh in Anthos.

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:49.310
The control plane in
Anthos is Google Manage.

00:39:49.310 --> 00:39:53.000
So what I showed was using
open source Istio Pilot

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:55.580
to actually configure
the Envoy proxies.

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:57.380
Today we announced
Traffic Director, which

00:39:57.380 --> 00:40:01.590
is the Google managed pilot is
a helpful way to think about it.

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:04.010
But it works just
the same way as Pilot

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:10.220
in that it uses the Envoy XES
APIs to configure the proxies.

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:13.120
So I hope that
clarifies the things

00:40:13.120 --> 00:40:15.670
if you're kind of a bit confused
about how all the pieces fit

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:16.170
together.

00:40:18.570 --> 00:40:21.270
I want to also say that
this is not the end.

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:25.200
There are multiple other
Istio talks this week at NEXT.

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:27.690
A quick callout that I'm
giving another talk tomorrow

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:32.340
morning at 9:00 AM in the AMC
[? Metreon, ?] Debugging Istio.

00:40:32.340 --> 00:40:33.970
And it's almost entirely demos.

00:40:33.970 --> 00:40:35.550
So if this was not
enough for you,

00:40:35.550 --> 00:40:38.520
and you want a whole other
hour of this in your week,

00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:41.360
you can join us
tomorrow morning.

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:44.700
One final callout that
we here at Google,

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.820
we are conducting
some user studies

00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:48.710
for folks who are
either not using Istio

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:51.620
or are currently
trying to use Istio.

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:54.250
And if you would like to
join these user studies

00:40:54.250 --> 00:41:01.710
and to provide feedback in
any way, go to the form here.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:03.500
So we are at nine minutes.

00:41:03.500 --> 00:41:06.440
I would love to answer some
live questions in the remaining

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:07.020
time.

00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:09.230
I do want to say that if
I'm not able to answer

00:41:09.230 --> 00:41:11.540
your question right now,
I'm happy to talk after.

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:13.850
I will be around
outside the room.

00:41:13.850 --> 00:41:16.550
I'm not, I guess, and
all-knowing Istio guru,

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:18.300
although I really wish I was.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:20.300
So with that, anyone who
wants to ask a question

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:22.890
can just line up at those
mics right down the middle.

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:25.800
And I will try to
answer as best as I can.

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:26.510
Thank you.

